1. Direct API Wrapper Pattern 
 • The simplest approach. 
 • The agent calls external APIs directly through an MCP server.

2. Composite Service Pattern 
 • Unifies multiple APIs into a single service. 
 • No more juggling countless calls!

3. MCP-to-Agent Pattern 
 • Triggers tools via the MCP server for specialized reasoning. 
 • Outputs are handed off to specialized agents.

4. Event-Driven Integration Pattern 
 • Perfect for asynchronous workflows. 
 • Listens to event streams to trigger processes.

5. Configuration Use Pattern 
 • Manages or configures tools dynamically. 
 • Enables adaptive and self-tuning behaviors for agents.

6. Analytics Data Access Pattern 
 • Pulls real-time data from analytics systems. 
 • Informed decisions come from data-driven insights.

7. Hierarchical MCP Pattern 
 • Coordinates multiple domain-specific MCPs. 
 • Ideal for complex architectures requiring collaboration among sectors.

8. Local Resource Access Pattern 
 • Allows secure access to local files or on-device tools. 
 • Ensures secure file handling and local processing.


